tensions are escalating between two of  the biggest powers in the Middle East  Saudi Arabia and Iran Saudi Arabia  ordered all citizens to leave Lebanon  after the country's prime minister  mysteriously announced he's stepping  down he made that announcement while in  Saudi Arabia Lebanon is currently on the  front lines of a regional conflict  between Saudi Arabia and Iran Secretary  of State Rex Tillerson said the United  States has been assured that Prime  Minister Saad Hariri is not being held  against his will  Holly Williams is in Istanbul Turkey  Holly good morning good morning Saad al  Hariri resigns suddenly as Lebanon's  prime minister on Saturday while  visiting Saudi Arabia and in very  mysterious circumstances there have been  reports that Saudi Arabian authorities  forced him to quit his job and he's  being held under house arrest those  reports have not been officially  confirmed and the Saudis insists that  al-hariri is a free man we simply do not  know exactly what's happened but the  reason this matters is that Saudi Arabia  and Iran are fighting a proxy war in the  Middle East vying for power in countries  including Syria and Yemen Iran has  enormous influence in new in Lebanon via  Hezbollah a group the u.s. considers a  terrorist organization Saudi Arabia  could now be challenging that influence  and there are fears it'll spark a  conflict in Lebanon some in this part of  the world believe that Saudi Arabia has  been emboldened by support from  President Trump who recently tweeted  great confidence in the Saudi leadership  all of this comes against the backdrop  of the rise to power of Saudi Arabia's  Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman who at  just 32 years old is consolidating power  in his hands  including with an anti-corruption  campaign that's already seen more than  200 people detained reportedly including  several members of the royal family  Gael all right thank you very much Holly  Williams
 

